include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: a loop whose bound is wider than the backing it indexes.
--
-- `block` is 64 bytes and the loop runs `i` to 100. Nothing about any single
-- line is wrong -- the access is `[block + i : 1]`, which is what every scan
-- loop in the corpus looks like -- and the mistake is only visible by putting
-- the loop's bound next to the array's size.
--
-- Which is what the compiler does. The bound is read off the loop, the index is
-- an induction variable stepping by one, and the backing's size is the number
-- the array is declared with, so the last pass is decided rather than guessed.
--
-- GCC does not report this. It has value-range propagation and object sizes and
-- could in principle, but stays silent at `-Wall -Wextra -Warray-bounds=2
-- -Wstringop-overflow=4 -fanalyzer` -- checked, with a live loop and the buffer
-- written first so an uninitialised-value finding could not stand in for a
-- bounds one.

program goes
  block is 64 bytes
  word is 8 bytes
  n is 0
  i is 0
  t is 0
  input is already linux.file (descriptor is 0)
  terminal is already linux.file (descriptor is 1)
  input.read (buffer is block, capacity is 64, count is n)
  scan goes
    leave scan when i >= 100
    t is t + [block + i : 1]
    i is i + 1
    repeat scan
  end
  [word + 0 : 8] is t
  terminal.write (buffer is word, count is 8)
end
